"Have you ever opened the door of a car that has been standing in the sun? The air inside is much hotter than the air outside. Sunlight went in through the windows and warmed the seats, and the heat could not get out again. Something a little like this happens to the whole Earth, and it is a good thing that it does. Start with the sunlight. Light from the sun passes easily through the air. About one third of it is bounced back to space by clouds, by the air, and by the ground. The rest is taken in, most of it by the ground and the sea, and it warms them. Anything warm gives off heat, and the Earth is no different. The warm ground sends its heat back toward space. But this heat travels as a kind of light that our eyes cannot see, and the air treats it differently from sunlight. Most of the air is nitrogen and oxygen, and they let this heat pass. But a few gases catch it. The most important one is water vapor. Carbon dioxide is another, and although it is only a tiny part of the air, it matters a great deal. A gas that catches heat sends it out again in all directions, so some of it comes back down to the ground. Clouds do the same thing. That is why a cloudy night stays warmer than a clear one. Scientists call this the greenhouse effect, and the gases that do it are called greenhouse gases. Without them, the average temperature at the surface of the Earth would be about −18°C. With them, it is about 15°C. That difference of 33 degrees is the reason we have liquid water, green plants, and a place to live. So the greenhouse effect is not a bad thing. It is a blanket, and the Earth needs one. But the thickness of the blanket matters. The planet Venus has air that is almost entirely carbon dioxide, and its surface is about 464°C. On Earth, the amount of carbon dioxide in the air has been measured carefully every year since 1958, and it has been rising. That is why scientists watch this number so closely.": "9f971b58f2c9",
